And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.820
Oh, hi there.

9
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.060
How are you?

10
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.220
I am well.

11
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.000
How is New York City?

12
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.700
It is great.

13
00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.320
You know, I just love that we can do the podcast from two different cities.

14
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.280
We're bi-coastal office ladies.

15
00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.000
So cool.

16
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.800
I've got my sound engineer here, Casey, and we are ready to go.

17
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:45.700
I cannot believe we are on the second-to-last episode of Season 6.

18
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.080
Me either.

19
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.480
It's crazy.

20
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.580
It's crazy.

21
00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.560
This is The Chump.

22
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.660
It is Season 6, Episode 25, written by Erin Schur and directed by Randall Einhorn.

23
00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.640
Give us a summary, Jenna.

24
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:00.740
Okay.

25
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.040
Since you asked.

26
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.620
Here's your summary.

27
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:14.560
Last week, Michael learned that Donna, the woman he's been seeing, is married and is cheating on her husband with Michael.

28
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.920
But Michael does not call off the relationship.

29
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.620
And he receives a lot of flack from his employees for continuing his affair with Donna.

30
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:32.740
Meanwhile, Pam and Jim struggle to stay awake at work after a late night with the baby, and Dwight and Angela are at odds over the validity of their co-parenting contract.

31
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:37.140
Oh, I have some good stuff about the Dwight-Angela co-parenting contract.

32
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.300
I'll wait till we get to it.

33
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:43.640
I was hoping you would, lady, because I think there's stories there.

34
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:44.620
Mm-hmm.

35
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.340
All right.

36
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.040
Fast fact number one.

37
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.940
This was our 125th episode of The Office.

38
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.260
Wow.

39
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:55.500
Yeah.

40
00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:04.160
We shot this episode the week of March 19th, 2010, and it was viewed by 6.6 million people.

41
00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.580
That seems like a lot of folks.

42
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.100
It was a lot of folks.

43
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.320
We were second in the ratings right behind Grey's Anatomy.

44
00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.440
They were a big competition at this time.

45
00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.980
I mean, it was a big deal if you were even holding your own with Grey's Anatomy.

46
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:19.760
I know.

47
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.380
I mean, they were always number one.

48
00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.900
Now, the reviews for this episode were pretty good.

49
00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:33.480
The AV Club gave it a B+, and it ranked as the 10th most well-liked episode by fans for Season 6 on Office Tally.

50
00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:39.420
So, of all 26 episodes in Season 6, this was ranked 10th.

51
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.180
So, it was very well-liked.

52
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.420
I love a stat.

53
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.020
Oh, me too, lady.

54
00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.620
Now, the fan-favorite moment that was most mentioned in the comments was, from this episode, there was a moment.

55
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.440
Oh, I'm going to guess, I'm Beyonce always.

56
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.340
Oh, yes, that did get a lot of mentions.

57
00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.120
That was very popular.

58
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.540
That's my favorite line.

59
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.280
That's a mug right there.

60
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:03.880
Yeah.

61
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:15.060
No, the most popular moment was when Dwight and Angela are having sex in the warehouse, and it pans up to Pam and Jim listening from their sleeping spot.

62
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.160
That's hilarious.

63
00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.000
All right.

64
00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.460
Fast fact number two is a location breakdown.

65
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.140
We got a fan question from Harmjen V in the Netherlands.

66
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.260
Where did you film the baseball game, and who were the players?

67
00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:34.080
Good question.

68
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:34.880
Yeah.

69
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:43.280
I mean, a big portion of this episode takes place at a baseball field, with Michael and Andy watching Donna's husband, who is a high school baseball coach.

70
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.120
This was our first day of shooting.

71
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:53.820
We filmed on location at College of the Canyons on Rockwell Canyon Road in Santa Clarita, California.

72
00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.740
And then our base camp, which is where they put all the trailers, and they put the food, and hair and makeup, everything.

73
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:02.580
Yes.

74
00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:05.760
That was in an adjacent parking lot.

75
00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:07.560
It was parking lot number eight.

76
00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:15.000
So if you're a diehard fan, and you even want to tour our base camps, you can go to College of the Canyons parking lot eight.

77
00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:20.680
And that is where Steve Carell and Ed Helms had their lunch that day.

78
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:20.740
Okay.

79
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.500
So everyone you see in these scenes are background performers.

80
00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.360
Randy Cordray told me we had around 100 extras that day.

81
00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:30.980
For the baseball scenes?

82
00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:32.860
Yeah, for the baseball scenes.

83
00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.360
He said that they went to Central Casting.

84
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.720
You know Central Casting.

85
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.880
That's the famous background casting agency.

86
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.120
I was signed up with them when I first came to LA.

87
00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.200
I was signed up with them.

88
00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.020
You know my 90210 story.

89
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.160
Oh, yes.

90
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:45.780
Yeah.

91
00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:46.380
Exactly.

92
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:53.120
Well, Central Casting provided us with all of the spectators, and here is what we asked for.

93
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.580
We wanted six people who could be grandparents.

94
00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.240
We wanted 18 adults to be parents.

95
00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:10.100
And we needed 34 high school students, but all of the students had to be 18 years of age or older who looked younger, because then they could work adult hours.

96
00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:10.600
Right.

97
00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:17.320
But for the actual baseball scene, like the baseball players, we used a different company.

98
00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:22.100
I guess it's no longer in business, but it's called Smitty's Casting.

99
00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.880
Like Smitty with a Z.

100
00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.860
Smitty's Casting.

101
00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.300
And they were really playing.

102
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.240
Yeah, the players were really playing, and that's why we went to this casting place,

103
00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:40.100
because they're a specialty casting service that provides productions with sports-playing background performers.

104
00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:48.500
So, we got enough people to fill two baseball teams, the Scranton Coalhawks and the Wolverines.

105
00:05:49.300 --> 00:06:02.080
Smitty's Casting sent us two male umpires, one male assistant coach, three male Wolverines coaches, 18 male Coalhawk players, and 18 male Wolverine players.

106
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.720
And then our wardrobe department provided the team uniforMs.

107
00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.020
I would have signed up for Smitty's.

108
00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:10.380
Maybe they would have taken me as a tennis player.

109
00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:13.100
Yes, exactly.

110
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.680
Can you play at, like, an almost professional level?

111
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.380
Well, I, um, no, I can't.

112
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:21.660
But you know what?

113
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.200
I have a very good forehand.

114
00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:28.240
I think maybe you would just put that on your profile over at Central Casting.

115
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:31.220
I'm sorry to say I'm not sure you qualify for Smitty's.

116
00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.140
Thank you for calling.

117
00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.240
I don't make the Smitty's cut.

118
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:33.320
What?

119
00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:34.580
I'm so sorry.

120
00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:38.720
We are looking for professional level sports players.

121
00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:39.500
Wow.

122
00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.220
Did you ever play on a college team or at the state level?

123
00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.180
No, Jenna, but I did play.

124
00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:45.440
I'm sorry.

125
00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:46.000
I'm not Jenna.

126
00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.080
I'm the woman on the phone at Smitty's.

127
00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.060
Oh, no, Barbara at Smitty's.

128
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:51.100
Here's the thing.

129
00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:55.560
I was once told by my high school coach that I have a lovely country club game.

130
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:56.140
So.

131
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.200
Ma'am, I'm so sorry.

132
00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:02.120
I'm going to have to just forward you over to Central Casting.

133
00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.240
But congratulations on your terrific forehand.

134
00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.620
I hear some snark with Barbara.

135
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.760
Well, you've kept Barbara on the phone for a very long time.

136
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:16.100
She needs to move on to other calls that are actually going to produce a client.

137
00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:16.680
Oh, my gosh.

138
00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:18.100
Barbara at Smitty's.

139
00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:18.780
Listen.

140
00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.060
Listen, try being Barbara.

141
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:21.940
Okay.

142
00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:22.580
Not easy.

143
00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:24.440
It's not easy to be Barbara.

144
00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:25.860
Thank you for calling Smitty's.

145
00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:26.480
This is Barbara.

146
00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.240
Hey, so I would like to sign up for Smitty's.

147
00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:34.980
I play pool and also I can bowl very well.

148
00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:36.620
Are you professional level?

149
00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:38.740
How do you define professional?

150
00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:39.620
Clicking.

151
00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.060
Look at the sass when you're Barbara.

152
00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:42.960
Do you see what I'm saying?

153
00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:44.460
Look what you have to deal with.

154
00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:46.340
All right.

155
00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:46.960
Okay.

156
00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.660
Should we move on to fast fact number three?

157
00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:49.700
Let's do it.

158
00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:52.320
I'm calling fast fact number three.

159
00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:54.700
Is there a guest star in the house?

160
00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:56.620
Yes, there is.

161
00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:01.540
We have three new guest stars in this episode.

162
00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:02.980
And guess what?

163
00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.760
They have all appeared on the TV show House.

164
00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:08.220
Oh.

165
00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.460
That's why there's a guest star in the house.

166
00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.640
Oh, you're making a pun.

167
00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:14.160
I punned it.

168
00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:15.100
You punned it.

169
00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:21.500
So Amy Peetz is back as Donna, but this fast fact refers to our never-before-seen guest stars.

170
00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:26.460
For example, Bruno Oliver as our mediator, Mr. Barr.

171
00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:35.060
He has been on Arrested Development, Mad Men, Modern Family, most recently in the movie Birds of Prey, and House.

172
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.440
Bruno was so much fun, and he was just amazing.

173
00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:42.780
Like, I loved all of our scenes.

174
00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:50.120
As an actor, I have to imagine that was such a fun guest star to come on the show and work with you and Rain in those scenes.

175
00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:56.900
He was so good, too, as just the voice of reason between these two absolutely ridiculous people.

176
00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:58.920
He had some of my favorite lines.

177
00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:05.040
So our other guest star is Donna's husband, Shane, who was played by Larkin Campbell.

178
00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:13.660
Larkin has appeared in Passions, 24, Criminal Minds, Grey's Anatomy, our big competition, and also House.

179
00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:22.520
Finally, our guest star Tina Huang, who played the reporter at the end, who confronts Michael about the exploding printers.

180
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.540
She was a regular on the TV show Arrow.

181
00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.200
She also had a recurring role on Law & Order L.A.

182
00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.400
That was a short-lived spinoff of the Law & Order series.

183
00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:37.100
But I kid you not, she has also appeared on House.

184
00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:40.340
So there were guest stars in the House.

185
00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:41.660
Nice.

186
00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.720
Well, that's all I got for Fast Facts.

187
00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:48.980
But I was very tickled when I was looking up all of the actors' resumes, and they had all been in-house.

188
00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:49.800
Different seasons.

189
00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.720
Were you tickled by the fact that they had this crossover show or that you saw an opportunity to make a pun?

190
00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:56.880
Both, lady.

191
00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:03.600
Well, let's take a break, because when we come back, we're going to find a whole bunch of radon kits all over the office.

192
00:10:03.740 --> 00:10:05.100
Or are they ant traps?

193
00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:08.700
Or are they the little thing you turn upside down and it goes, moo?

194
00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:11.340
Or are they none of the above?

195
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:12.440
We'll be back.

196
00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.270
I think this might be Toby's happiest day.

197
00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:24.670
A hundred percent.

198
00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:26.690
Well, it starts as Toby's happiest day.

199
00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.470
Yes, absolutely.

200
00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:29.830
It starts that way.

201
00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:31.450
He's in the conference room.

202
00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:37.230
You guys, he is holding court about one of his favorite subjects, radon.

203
00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:50.600
One, he is explaining to everyone that he'll be placing radon kits all over the office, and he urges everyone, in particular Michael, and he's snapping at Michael,

204
00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.820
to please not throw them in the garbage.

205
00:10:52.820 --> 00:11:03.660
At 13 seconds after Toby is snapping at Michael repeatedly, I want you to look at Steve's face as Michael, the way he glares at Toby.

206
00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.320
I went back and looked in the script.

207
00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:08.340
You know this was in the cold open shooting draft for Secretary's Day.

208
00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:12.740
I had to know if this look to Toby was scripted.

209
00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:13.580
It was.

210
00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:17.280
It said, Michael gives Toby an annoyed look.

211
00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.760
And I want you to know, Jenna, we could not get through it.

212
00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:24.380
The first time Steve did it, we all erupted into laughter.

213
00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:25.600
It's in the bloopers.

214
00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:34.120
Well, we got a fan question, actually a fan mail flurry, asking, where is Pam during this cold open?

215
00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:36.520
Was Jenna not available for filming?

216
00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:37.560
Why am I not there?

217
00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.340
When I watched this cold open, I actually thought the same thing.

218
00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:45.140
And then I remembered, like you said, this cold open was written for Secretary's Day.

219
00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:50.880
We actually shot it that week, and Pam was not back from her maternity leave yet.

220
00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.460
She returns after the cold open, so that's why I'm not there.

221
00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.820
And that's why I did not get to see Steve's glare.

222
00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:58.420
Oh, yeah, that's right.

223
00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.260
Pam is on maternity leave.

224
00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.040
Well, that's a great catch.

225
00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:09.680
Michael is going to have this talking head, and he is going to walk around his office and talk about all of the radon test kits.

226
00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.040
The first radon kit, he mistook for an ant trap.

227
00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:16.780
Then, of course, he thought it was the thing that you turn over that makes the cow noise.

228
00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:19.240
And he just keeps tossing him in the trash.

229
00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.900
The last one he tossed in the trash just out of spite.

230
00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:28.720
Well, I asked Randy about these test kits, and it turns out these are not real radon test kits.

231
00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:30.780
Fakie test kit alert.

232
00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.360
Fakie radon test kit.

233
00:12:33.620 --> 00:12:34.100
Yeah.

234
00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:38.860
Randy said that the real ones at the time were actually pretty large and quite pricey.

235
00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:44.660
And we were looking for something smaller that really did kind of look more like an ant trap.

236
00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:53.600
So, Phil Shea bought some inexpensive just electronic modules at Fry's Electronics for Toby to place around the office.

237
00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:59.580
And after we got them, we realized that they looked like the thing that goes moo.

238
00:12:59.860 --> 00:13:03.640
So, that line was added later after our purchase was made.

239
00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:06.280
I was curious about that because it's so specific.

240
00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.920
And you know it's an instant memory.

241
00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:08.840
We all have.

242
00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.500
We've all seen those things.

243
00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:11.340
For sure.

244
00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.200
Angela, we got a piece of mail from Brent M.

245
00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:17.320
in Vancouver, Canada, who said,

